# The Relay: environment. Copy to .env.local (gitignored) and fill in.
#   cp .env.example .env.local
#
# run.sh sources .env.local automatically. The DEPLOYED server does not read
# either file: it is systemd that owns its environment, so anything below that
# matters in production belongs in the unit (Environment= or EnvironmentFile=).
#
# Nothing here is required to develop. Every default works out of the box, and
# mail prints to the terminal rather than being sent.

# --- developer doors (backend/devgate.py) ---------------------------------
# The design gallery at /preview.
RELAY_PREVIEW_KEY=1234567890
# God mode at /god (docs/GOD_MODE.md). Separate from the preview key on
# purpose: that one only exposes throwaway dummy matches, this one controls
# real ones. CHANGE THIS.
RELAY_GOD_KEY=change-me


# --- accounts (docs/ACCOUNTS.md) ------------------------------------------

# Where the accounts database lives. Relative paths resolve from the repo root.
# In production point this at storage that SURVIVES A REDEPLOY — if the file
# sits inside the checkout, a fresh clone silently deletes every account.
#RELAY_DB_PATH=/var/lib/relay/relay.db


# --- mail (backend/mailer.py) ---------------------------------------------

# THE ONE THAT BREAKS PRODUCTION IF YOU FORGET IT.
#
# Every link in every email is built from this: verification, password reset,
# sign-in links. Leave it unset with real mail configured and the server now
# refuses to start rather than mailing people links to their own computer.
#
# It is configuration and not the request's Host header on purpose — that
# header is attacker-controlled, and a reset link built from it is the standard
# way to make a server mail a working password reset to someone else's domain.
#
# Must be https in production: it is also what decides whether the session
# cookie is marked Secure.
#RELAY_BASE_URL=https://relay.example.com

# console (default) prints each mail to the server log, links included. That is
# the intended local workflow: sign up, read the link out of the terminal.
# Set to smtp to actually send.
#RELAY_MAIL_BACKEND=smtp

# The envelope sender. Its domain is the one that needs SPF and DKIM, and the
# one stamped into Message-ID.
#
# QUOTE IT. run.sh `source`s this file, and to a shell the < > around the
# address are redirections: unquoted, the line dies and the variable silently
# stays unset. systemd parses EnvironmentFile itself and does not mind either
# way, so the mistake only shows up locally.
#RELAY_MAIL_FROM="The Relay <no-reply@relay.example.com>"

#RELAY_SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
#RELAY_SMTP_PORT=587
#RELAY_SMTP_USER=relay@example.com
#RELAY_SMTP_PASSWORD=

# How to encrypt the connection: starttls | ssl | none.
# Left unset it follows the port, which is right for almost everyone:
#   587 (submission)    -> starttls
#   465 (implicit TLS)  -> ssl
# Getting these the wrong way round is the most common reason a correct set of
# credentials refuses to send, because the symptom is a hang, not an error.
#
# "none" is for a local mail catcher (Mailpit, MailHog) and is never inferred.
# It refuses to run with a password set, rather than putting one on the wire in
# clear text.
#RELAY_SMTP_SECURITY=starttls


# --- deliverability: the part that is not code ----------------------------
#
# Credentials alone do not get mail delivered. Gmail rejects outright:
#
#   550 5.7.26 Gmail requires all senders to authenticate with either SPF or DKIM
#
# So the domain in RELAY_MAIL_FROM needs, in its DNS:
#
#   SPF    a TXT record naming whoever sends for you
#          e.g. "v=spf1 include:_spf.google.com ~all"
#   DKIM   the signing key your provider gives you
#   DMARC  a TXT record at _dmarc.<domain>, even just "v=DMARC1; p=none"
#
# The quickest route is a transactional provider (Resend, Postmark, SendGrid,
# Mailgun): make an account, verify the domain by pasting the DNS records they
# give you, and use their SMTP credentials above. Sending straight from this
# box will not work — a residential or cloud IP with no PTR record is refused
# or filed as spam whatever the headers say.
